  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Comprehensive Multi-Sectoral Assistance To Internally Displaced Persons and Host Communities in Yemen</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">After more than eight years of conflict, the humanitarian needs continue to worsen across Yemen, especially for families living in protracted displacement and close to frontlines. Based on an Area Assessment (Mobility Tracking) conducted from July to September 2023, the International Organization for Migration (IOM)&#x2019;s Displacement Tracking Matrix (DTM) estimates there are 2,791,603 internally displaced persons (IDPs) and 1,878,906 IDP returnees in 13 governorates in the south of Yemen. Conflict-related reasons were still reported as a main driver of displacement and the needs of IDPs were aggravated by a weakened economy, rains, flooding and fuel shortages. IOM is contributing towards the alleviation of immediate suffering of food insecure populations in Yemen with timely and high impact multi-sectoral interventions, as well as complementary interventions that are crucial for informing the integrated response. This includes assessments through the DTM, providing water, sanitation and hygiene solutions, multi-purpose cash assistance, healthcare assistance, shelter assistance, non-food items, nutrition services; strengthening social and protection structures to mitigate, prevent and respond to risks and reduce reliance on negative coping mechanisms; and enhancing site coordination, community participation and care/maintenance at IDP collective sites.</narrative>
  </description>
